Adaptive Cards do not directly support changing the background color of the entire card. However, you can achieve a similar effect using several workarounds:

**1. Using a Container with a Background Color:**

This is the most common and recommended approach.  You wrap the content of your Adaptive Card within a `Container` element and set the `background` property of the `Container`.

```json
{
  "type": "AdaptiveCard",
  "body": [
    {
      "type": "Container",
      "background": "blue",
      "padding": "8px",
      "wrap": true,
      "items": [
        {
          "type": "TextBlock",
          "text": "Hello, Adaptive Card!",
          "size": "Large"
        },
        {
          "type": "TextBlock",
          "text": "This card has a blue background."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
```

* **`background`:**  Sets the background color.  You can use color names (e.g., "blue", "red", "green"), hex codes (e.g., "#FF0000"), or RGB values (e.g., "rgb(255, 0, 0)").
* **`padding`:**  Adds padding around the content within the container.  This prevents the content from touching the edges of the background color.
* **`wrap`:**  Ensures that the content within the container wraps to fit the available space.  This is important for responsive layouts.
* **`items`:**  Contains the elements you want to display within the container.

**2. Using a Stack with a Background Color:**

Similar to the `Container`, you can use a `Stack` element with a background color.  `Stack` is useful when you want to arrange elements vertically or horizontally.

```json
{
  "type": "AdaptiveCard",
  "body": [
    {
      "type": "Stack",
      "direction": "vertical", // or "horizontal"
      "background": "green",
      "padding": "8px",
      "wrap": true,
      "items": [
        {
          "type": "TextBlock",
          "text": "Stack with Green Background"
        },
        {
          "type": "Image",
          "url": "https://example.com/image.png"
